ory (chart labels count).\n\nNow let's determine what local can compute for lifespan that matches the chart's labels. The chart's average age at death 11 (All) and 9 (Bottlenose), max 61, average age living 18 (All), 15 (Bottlenose). Could the local data reproduce these as median? All dead median 7, not 11. Bottlenose 7. Mean of all dead with age definition including missing as age at capture? Let's try to find a definition that yields 11 and 9 simultaneously and is consistent.\n\nMaybe the age variable in lifespan.csv is not age at death but \"age at death in captivity\" computed as:\n- If born in captivity: statusDate - originDate (age since birth).\n- If captured: 0? Or age at capture unknown.\nThe story says \"average age at death\" without specifying true age vs captive age. The legend label \"maximum age at death in captivity\" suggests these are ages at death (true ages), and max 61 is true age (animal captured as calf?).\n\nLet's examine story lifespan.csv all dead mean 11.14 exactly. The label says 11, so it is mean of that distribution. Bottlenose dead mean 11.21, but label 9 — inconsistent. Unless the Bottlenose label's \"data age 9\" is just annotation position, not the mean. The label text \"average age at death\" might be placed at a bar near age 9 but the actual mean 11.2. That would be bizarre; maybe the author made an error or placed label at 9 to avoid overlapping.\n\nLet's re-read: In `generateAnnotationData`, Bottlenose average age at death = {age: 9, count: -23}. They hard-coded approximate position. It may not equal the actual mean; annotations in d3 are placed at data points. Could be they computed average as 11 but placed at a tall bar? No, they'd place at (11, count at 11 = -30). The count -23 corresponds to age 9's bar (-23). So the label is attached to the age-9 bar, not to mean. Maybe they computed \"average age at death\" from a different subset (e.g., only captive-born Bottlenose) yielding 9. Let's check local captive-born Bottlenose de